Title:
LACUNA Chain: Ghost Frames — defeats all EDR layers of call-stack-based detection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
This post presents “LACUNA Chain,” a set of Windows x64 call-stack spoofing techniques designed to evade modern EDR telemetry that captures stacks at the kernel boundary (e.g., ETW-Ti STACKWALK and kernel callback-driven stack walks). It focuses on making kernel-collected call stacks “lie” by exploiting how `RtlVirtualUnwind` treats instruction pointers that fall outside `.pdata` `RUNTIME_FUNCTION` coverage (treated as leaf frames that advance `RSP` by 8). Key contributions include BYOUD-Gap (zero `.pdata` modification spoofing), an ETW-Ti APC timing window to control when stack snapshots occur, and “ghost” code regions/gadgets inside signed DLLs (ntdll/kernelbase/wow64) plus a win32u NOP-gap chain for stable whitelisted frames. It also extends parameter encryption (decrypt-at-syscall via HW breakpoint + VEH) to reduce user-mode parameter visibility while stacks are being spoofed. The work is primarily useful for red teams/malware developers building stealthy injection/execution chains, and for defenders designing new detections (e.g., flagging stack frames that land in `.pdata` gaps). It’s interesting because it targets the post-2024 shift from userland hooks to kernel stack-based detections and shows practical bypasses even under CET shadow stack constraints.

Technical Focus:
- Windows x64 stack unwinding internals (`RtlVirtualUnwind`, `.pdata`, `RUNTIME_FUNCTION`, `UNWIND_CODE`)
- Call-stack spoofing via `.pdata` gaps (“ghost frames”) and semantic proximity to real APIs
- ETW-Ti (Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence) STACKWALK behavior and APC-based collection timing
- CET shadow stack implications and CET-compatible spoofing approaches (BYOUD variants)
- Hardware breakpoints + VEH for last-moment syscall parameter decryption
- Gadget/byte-level binary analysis of system DLLs (ntdll, kernelbase, win32u, wow64)

Use Cases:
- Building CET-compatible call-stack spoofing for syscall-based injection chains
- Evading ETW-Ti STACKWALK attribution for `NtAllocateVirtualMemory`/`NtProtectVirtualMemory`/APC injection sequences
- Creating “legitimate-looking” stacks using signed-module frames (ntdll/kernelbase/win32u/wow64) without `.pdata` tampering
- Researching/detecting anomalous stack frames that fall into `.pdata` uncovered ranges (gap-address detection)
- Developing PoCs to evaluate EDR reliance on stack-based rules vs kernel callback correlation

Keywords:
LACUNA Chain, BYOUD-Gap, BYOUD-RT, BYOUD-MF, call stack spoofing, ghost frames, `.pdata` gaps, RUNTIME_FUNCTION, UNWIND_INFO, RtlVirtualUnwind, RtlLookupFunctionEntry, ETW-Ti, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, STACKWALK, USER_APC, alertable wait, CET, shadow stack, win32u.dll, ntdll.dll, kernelbase.dll, wow64.dll, KiUserExceptionDispatcher, ObRegisterCallbacks, Sysmon Event ID 10, hardware breakpoints, VEH, indirect syscalls